WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.360
Detection logic should be understandable and maintainable.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.100
Clear naming conventions, readable conditions, consistent severity labels,

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.010
and descriptive metadata help analysts and future maintainers understand what the rule is

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:11.930
meant to do.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:15.780
Documentation should explain the behavior being detected, the data sources required,

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.955
key assumptions, expected false positives, known limitations,

00:00:18.955 --> 00:00:20.560
and recommended triage steps.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.320
It should also point to relevant references or internal tickets when useful.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.758
Readable logic matters because detections change over time.

00:00:26.758 --> 00:00:31.595
A rule may need tuning after a false positive, a telemetry change, a new business process,

00:00:31.595 --> 00:00:34.160
a platform migration, or a lesson from an incident.

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:37.200
If nobody can understand the rule, nobody can improve it safely.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.440
Good rule writing also supports collaboration.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.400
Analysts, threat hunters, incident responders, engineers,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.265
and managers may all need to understand why a detection exists and how to interpret its

00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:46.760
output.
